<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Long-stay Tourists: Ownership Paperwork Questions for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>A long-stay tourist buying a motorbike in Hanoi is buying metal with a legal shadow attached, and the shadow is the part that follows the name home. Ownership paperwork is where visitor purchases quietly succeed or loudly collapse, and it deserves the same patient walk-around the machine itself receives, performed in the buying week rather than the packing week. This guide collects the questions, holds every answer to the standard of current official verification instead of remembered folklore, and points at the one desk in the city where the whole map can be sanity-checked by people with nothing to sell.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk briefs visitors on both sides of the paperwork question honestly, because the shop rents machines and never sells them. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
